  "articleSummary": "**Context Summary for Translation Model:**\n\n**Thesis & Key Points:** The article argues that the agentic AI threat is not sci-fi hype requiring a singular breakthrough, but an immediate reality driven by the multiplication of two compounding factors: autonomous task endurance (measured by METR benchmarks) and orchestration breadth (deploying swarms of sub-agents via tools like Claude Code). Traditional capability gates and economic barriers fail to stop attackers because of \"LLMjacking\"—stealing victim cloud credentials so the attacker operates for free while the victim pays the compute bill. This asymmetry enables relentless brute-force attacks where per-attempt costs approach zero for attackers, overwhelming linearly scaled human defensive reviews. \n\n**Tone & Audience:** The tone is an analytical, urgent technical critique aimed at cybersecurity teams, system architects, and engineering leadership who dismiss AI agent threats as overhyped. \n\n**Framing & Metaphors:** The text heavily uses *The Terminator* / *Skynet* franchise as a recurring framing device (specifically Kyle Reese's quote \"absolutely will not stop,\" repurposed here as \"absolutely will not stop billing\"), alongside economic metaphors comparing attacker compute endurance to an unmetered meter running on someone else's credit card.",
